// @run
// @log 1
// @warning Unused variable `u`
// The reported program, verbatim — no `is`, no narrowing, nothing but a module global whose
// annotation is a union with a generic-application member. On master `vl check` reported
// nothing worse than an unused-variable WARNING, `vl build` wrote a 173-byte module, and
// that module failed to parse as WebAssembly:
//
//   (global (mut (ref 2)) i32.const 1 struct.new 0)     -- the defective bytes
//   (global (mut (ref 2)) i32.const 1 i32.const 1 struct.new 0 struct.new 2)  -- the control
//
// where type 2 is the union box `(struct (field i32) (field anyref))` and type 0 the plain
// `{v: i32}` struct: the cell was declared as the box and the initializer built the payload
// alone. The non-generic control (`type Plain = {v:i32}; type U = Plain | Tag`) emits the
// second line and prints 1, which is what isolated the generic APPLICATION as the trigger.
//
// Kept separate from the narrowing pin next to it because that one stops at the `is` reject
// on master — only this spelling reaches the silent invalid module.
type Box<T> = { v: T }
type Tag = { t: i32 }
type U = Box<i32> | Tag
const u: U = { v: 1 }
print(1)
